{
  "executive_summary": "Ridgeway closes Q2 with its product launch and CAC reduction objectives both in a worse position than the tracker colors suggest. The headline problem is KR 1.3: the DTC channel was redefined mid-quarter from a live e-commerce checkout to a waitlist-only beta — a scope change that makes the original target unmet regardless of the beta's readiness. This redefinition directly undermines KR 2.1 (CAC reduction), which explicitly depended on DTC being live for attribution efficiency. Both KRs should be assessed against their original definitions, and the scope change requires a formal leadership acknowledgment before Q3 planning can proceed. Two additional KRs require attention: KR 1.2 (retail revenue) ends the quarter at pace ratio 0.45, materially below target, with a decelerating trend that has no documented explanation; and KR 3.3 (patent filings) has had no update in seven weeks and is a measurement gap.",
  "leadership_decisions": [
    {
      "issue": "KR 1.3 scope redefinition: the DTC 'launch' was changed from live checkout to waitlist-only beta without formal leadership approval.",
      "affected_objectives": "Objective 1 (Nourish launch), Objective 2 (CAC reduction — KR 2.1 depends on DTC being live)",
      "decision_requested": "Leadership must formally acknowledge the scope change and decide: (a) count the beta as partial credit and set a hard Q3 date for full checkout launch, or (b) record KR 1.3 as missed in Q2 and carry the full checkout target into Q3 with no credit claimed. This decision also determines how to treat KR 2.1's Q2 result, since the CAC target assumed DTC would contribute 40% lower CAC during Q2.",
      "owner": "Beatrice Holloway and Marcus Webb, joint decision required by 2026-07-08"
    },
    {
      "issue": "KR 1.2 retail revenue deceleration: $0.28M in the final 4 weeks vs. $0.80M in the first 9 weeks — a sharp slowdown with no documented cause.",
      "affected_objectives": "Objective 1 (Nourish launch)",
      "decision_requested": "Holloway to brief leadership on the deceleration driver before Q3 planning. If the slowdown reflects a sell-in vs. sell-through lag (stores stocked but consumers not buying), leadership needs to decide whether to fund a Q3 consumer pull campaign or accept a slower ramp. If the slowdown reflects a distribution or pricing issue, that is a different intervention.",
      "owner": "Beatrice Holloway, analysis due 2026-07-10"
    }
  ],
  "at_risk_table": [
    {
      "kr": "KR 1.2 — Retail sell-through revenue",
      "owner": "Beatrice Holloway",
      "pace_ratio_or_progress": "45% at quarter close; pace ratio 0.45/1.0 = 0.45 — Off-track",
      "blocker_type": "Unknown deceleration — no analyst note explaining weeks 10-13 slowdown",
      "what_would_unblock": "Identify root cause (consumer pull vs. distribution vs. pricing) before Q3 spend decisions"
    },
    {
      "kr": "KR 1.3 — DTC channel launch",
      "owner": "Beatrice Holloway",
      "pace_ratio_or_progress": "0% against original target (live checkout); scope redefined mid-quarter without formal approval",
